  <section class="faq" id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Questions, answered</p>
        <h2>What longtime owners ask us first.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <details><summary>When is the right time to downsize in Los Angeles?</summary><p>The right time is usually when the home is costing you more than it gives back, in upkeep, stairs, taxes, or empty rooms. In Los Angeles, longtime owners often hold large amounts of equity, so timing is less about the market and more about your life. We help you weigh readiness, tax exposure, and pricing so the decision is made on your terms, not a deadline.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I take my property taxes with me when I downsize in California?</summary><p>Often, yes. If you're 55 or older, Prop 19 lets you transfer your low Prop 13 tax base (a base year value transfer) to a replacement home anywhere in California, up to three times. If the new home costs more, your taxable value rises only by the difference. The replacement home generally has to be bought or built within two years of your sale, so plan before you list. The rules are strict, so talk to a tax professional or your agent first. This is general information, not tax advice.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Isn't it smarter to wait for a better market?</summary><p>Not always. While you wait, you're paying insurance, utilities, and maintenance, often thousands of dollars a month, on space you're not using. Waiting six months for a theoretical higher price can quietly cost tens of thousands in carrying costs. We run your actual numbers so the decision is based on math, not hope.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Will I owe capital gains tax when I sell my house in California?</summary><p>Possibly. If you've owned and lived in the home for at least 2 of the last 5 years, a single homeowner can exclude up to $250,000 of gain and a married couple up to $500,000, but longtime LA owners frequently exceed that after decades of appreciation. The exact number depends on your cost basis and improvements, so we coordinate early with your CPA and build it into the plan. This is general information, not tax advice.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if my house needs work before selling?</summary><p>Most longtime homes do, and that's fine. We identify only the improvements that actually return more than they cost, arrange and manage the vendors, and can often defer payment until closing. You don't front cash or chase contractors.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do you handle a lifetime of belongings?</summary><p>We coordinate sorting, donation, estate sale, and clear-out as part of the process, on your pace. Nothing leaves without your say. The goal is that you move once, into a home that already fits, without the boxes and the overwhelm.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do you help with a move into senior living or a care community?</summary><p>Yes. Beyond selling the home, we guide seniors and their families through moves into care communities, with compassion, trusted resources, and zero added stress. It's one of the core reasons families call us.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I have to move out before you sell?</summary><p>No. We build the plan around your timeline, including options that let you sell first and move second, or secure your next place before listing. You should never feel homeless in the gap.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do you help sell a house in Studio City, Encino, or the Hollywood Hills?</summary><p>Yes. We sell homes in Studio City, Sherman Oaks, Encino, the Hollywood Hills, Beachwood Canyon, West Hollywood, Beverly Hills, Los Feliz, and across the Westside. We go deep on these neighborhoods rather than wide, so your home is priced by someone who knows the street.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What's the difference between a trust sale and a probate sale in Los Angeles?</summary><p>A trust sale happens when the home is held in a living trust, and the successor trustee can sell it according to the trust documents, without court supervision. A probate sale happens when there is no trust: the executor or administrator needs authority first, and the court may need to approve the sale. We handle both as trust and probate sale specialists in Los Angeles, working alongside your attorney so nothing stalls.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can you sell a house in probate in California?</summary><p>Yes. Once the court appoints an executor or administrator, the home can be sold. With full authority under the Independent Administration of Estates Act, a sale can often close without court confirmation. Without it, the court confirms the sale and other buyers can overbid. As your probate realtor in Los Angeles, we work alongside your probate attorney so the sale stays on track.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Will I owe taxes if I sell an inherited house in California?</summary><p>Usually little or no capital gains tax. Heirs generally receive a stepped-up basis equal to the home's value at the date of death, so only growth after that is taxed. The bigger issue is property tax: under Prop 19, an inherited home can be reassessed to current market value unless a child makes it their primary residence. Confirm the details with your CPA. This is general information, not tax advice.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I sell an inherited home in Los Angeles as-is?</summary><p>Yes. Heirs and trustees are never required to renovate before selling. We look at the property and tell you honestly whether a few targeted updates would return more than they cost, or whether selling as-is nets you the most with zero effort. Either way, you don't front cash or manage contractors.</p></details>
      </div>
